      "msg_057d9eb180a8d853016ae2205516ac8195a3d6a0babde7ff76": {
        "id": "msg_057d9eb180a8d853016ae2205516ac8195a3d6a0babde7ff76",
        "message": {
          "id": "msg_057d9eb180a8d853016ae2205516ac8195a3d6a0babde7ff76",
          "author": {
            "role": "assistant",
            "name": null,
            "metadata": {}
          },
          "create_time": 1793204309.16,
          "update_time": 1793204325.543,
          "content": {
            "content_type": "text",
            "parts": [
              "The lower limit \\(1\\) came from my switching back to the original cosine representation: **\\(u\\ge0\\) corresponds to \\(r=e^{2u}\\ge1\\)**. The whole-line exponential representation uses all \\(0<r<\\infty\\). I should have explained that switch.\n\nWe can retain the full three-dimensional space and the exponential kernel. Put\n\\[\nr=|X|,\\qquad L=\\log r,\\qquad\nW_{t,z}(r)=e^{tL^2/4}r^{-1/2+iz/2}.\n\\]\nOur starting identity is\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nH(t,z)=\\frac1{64\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\n(\\Delta_3\\Theta)(|X|)\\,W_{t,z}(|X|)\\,dX.\n}\n\\]\nHere \\(\\Delta_3\\Theta=\\Theta''+2\\Theta'/r\\) is the ordinary radial derivative for \\(r>0\\).\n\nTo calculate the differentiated kernel, write \\(W=r^{-1/2}v(L)\\). Then\n\\[\n\\Delta_3W=r^{-5/2}\\left(v''-\\frac14v\\right).\n\\]\nFor \\(v(L)=e^{tL^2/4+izL/2}\\),\n\\[\nv''=\\left[\\frac t2+\\frac{(tL+iz)^2}{4}\\right]v.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\Delta_3W_{t,z}(r)=\n\\frac{e^{tL^2/4}r^{-5/2+iz/2}}4\n\\left[t^2L^2+2itzL+2t-z^2-1\\right].\n}\n\\]\n\nThere is an endpoint issue when transferring this Laplacian from \\(\\Theta\\) to the kernel: \\(\\Theta(r)\\sim1/r\\) at zero and tends to \\(1\\) at infinity. For a formula valid for every real \\(t\\), we subtract those explicit backgrounds:\n\\[\n\\Theta(r)-\\max\\{1,r^{-1}\\}\n=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\Theta(r)-r^{-1},&0<r<1,\\\\\n\\Theta(r)-1,&r\\ge1.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThis difference decays rapidly at both ends.\n\nThe resulting **full-space kernel transform** is\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nH(t,z)=\\frac1{16}\n+\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\n\\left[\\Theta(|X|)-\\max\\{1,|X|^{-1}\\}\\right]\n\\mathcal K_t(z,X)\\,dX,\n}\n\\]\nwhere, explicitly,\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\mathcal K_t(z,X)=\n\\frac{e^{\\frac t4(\\log|X|)^2}|X|^{-5/2+iz/2}}{256\\pi}\n\\left[\nt^2(\\log|X|)^2+2itz\\log|X|+2t-z^2-1\n\\right].\n}\n\\]\nThis integral converges absolutely for every real \\(t\\) and complex \\(z\\).\n\nThe constant \\(1/16\\) is still required. The subtracted profile is continuous at \\(r=1\\), but its radial derivative jumps by \\(-1\\). Green’s identity therefore contributes the unit-sphere integral\n\\[\n\\frac1{64\\pi}\\int_{|X|=1}W_{t,z}(1)\\,dS\n=\\frac{4\\pi}{64\\pi}=\\frac1{16}.\n\\]\n\nThus the choice is now explicit: retain \\(\\Delta_3\\Theta\\) in the original whole-space integral, or move the Laplacian onto this calculated kernel and retain the background subtraction and surface contribution.\n\nI added the derivation, including the expanded exterior cosine kernel, to the [working change note](/Users/ebbaker/Documents/shifted-zeta-positivity/papers/investigations-5/05_radial_theta_collision_geometry/book/changes/01_GENERATING_FUNCTIONS_AND_KERNEL_CHANGE_WORKING_NOTE_20261028.md). The manuscript remains unchanged."
            ]
          },
