          "create_time": 1793204980.253,
          "update_time": 1793204984.523,
          "content": {
            "content_type": "text",
            "parts": [
              "Two simplifications fit together well: the smooth theta potential gives a cleaner Euclidean integral, and multiplying it by \\(\\sqrt r\\) turns inversion into ordinary reflection in \\(u=\\tfrac12\\log r\\). In those logarithmic coordinates, the radial operator becomes \\(1-\\partial_u^2\\). I’m recording both forms, along with the tradeoff between a smooth generator and an integral that converges for every complex \\(z\\)."
            ]
